			<section data-type="chapter" class="standard post-136 chapter type-chapter status-publish hentry">
		<header>
				<h1 class="entry-title">
			<span>21</span> Blackcap		</h1>
										</header>
		<p><span style="color: #339966"><strong>Names</strong></span></p>
<p><strong>Common name</strong> &#8211; Blackcap</p>
<p><strong>Scientific name</strong> &#8211; <em>Rubus leucodermis</em></p>
<p><strong>Other names</strong> &#8211; Whitebark Raspberry; Black Raspberry</p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-454 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-7-300x142.jpg" alt="" width="300" height="142" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-7-300x142.jpg 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-7-1024x485.jpg 1024w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-7-768x364.jpg 768w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-7-65x31.jpg 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-7-225x107.jpg 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-7-350x166.jpg 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-7.jpg 1430w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-455 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-4-300x142.jpg" alt="" width="300" height="142" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-4-300x142.jpg 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-4-1024x485.jpg 1024w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-4-768x364.jpg 768w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-4-65x31.jpg 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-4-225x107.jpg 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-4-350x166.jpg 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-4.jpg 1430w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-456 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-3-142x300.jpg" alt="" width="142" height="300" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-3-142x300.jpg 142w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-3-485x1024.jpg 485w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-3-768x1623.jpg 768w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-3-727x1536.jpg 727w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-3-65x137.jpg 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-3-225x475.jpg 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-3-350x740.jpg 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-3.jpg 937w" sizes="(max-width: 142px) 100vw, 142px" /></p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-457 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture4-3-300x200.png" alt="" width="300" height="200" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture4-3-300x200.png 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture4-3-65x43.png 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture4-3-225x150.png 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture4-3-350x233.png 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture4-3.png 624w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-458 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture5-1-224x300.jpg" alt="" width="224" height="300" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture5-1-224x300.jpg 224w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture5-1-65x87.jpg 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture5-1-225x301.jpg 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture5-1-350x469.jpg 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture5-1.jpg 504w" sizes="(max-width: 224px) 100vw, 224px" /></p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-459 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture6-300x200.png" alt="" width="300" height="200" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture6-300x200.png 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture6-65x43.png 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture6-225x150.png 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture6-350x233.png 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture6.png 624w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-460 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture7-300x200.jpg" alt="" width="300" height="200" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture7-300x200.jpg 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture7-65x43.jpg 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture7-225x150.jpg 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture7-350x233.jpg 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture7.jpg 468w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></p>
<p><span style="color: #339966"><strong>General information</strong></span></p>
<p style="text-align: justify">The traditional knowledge of Blackcap&#8217;s medicinal properties, particularly for wound healing and pain relief, aligns well with modern scientific findings showing significant anti-inflammatory, antimicrobial, and tissue-protective effects of its anthocyanins and ellagitannins. The round-shaped fruit is a 12–15 mm diameter aggregation of drupelets.</p>
<h3><span style="color: #339966"><strong>Traditional Indigenous Uses</strong></span></h3>
<p style="text-align: justify">Blackcap is a healing plant that gave its medicine through both its bark and its berries. The leaves and bark were often chewed and applied directly to wounds or burns to draw out pain and speed healing. When a wound festered or became infected, poultices made from the bark helped to cleanse and close it, while boiled bark teas were used to calm inflammation and ease the aches that lingered in the body.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify">During childbirth, the bark was sometimes boiled in seawater and drunk to help ease labor pains and support the mother through delivery. The same bark, when pounded and applied to the gums, brought relief from toothaches. The leaves, when steeped into tea, provided strength and nourishment, serving as a source of vitamin C during long winters when fresh food was scarce.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify">The berries themselves were both food and medicine, eaten to settle the stomach, aid digestion, and restore energy.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline"><em>Additional Traditional Uses</em></span></p>
<ul>
<li>Young shoots were harvested in spring, peeled, and eaten raw or cooked like asparagus</li>
<li>Berries were consumed fresh or dried</li>
<li>Used to make purple dye for ceremonial and practical purposes</li>
</ul>
<h3><span style="color: #339966"><strong>Biochemical Basis of Medicinal Properties</strong></span></h3>
<p><em>Key Bioactive Compounds</em></p>
<ol>
<li><strong> Anthocyanins</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>The major anthocyanins in Blackcap berries include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Cyanidin-3-O-glucoside (C3G)</li>
<li>Cyanidin-3-O-rutinoside (C3R)</li>
<li>Cyanidin-3-O-sophoroside</li>
<li>Cyanidin-3-O-(2&#8243;-O-glucosyl) rutinoside</li>
<li>Pelargonidin-3-O-glucoside</li>
</ul>
<p style="text-align: center">Chemical Structure of Cyanidin-3-O-glucoside (C3G)</p>
<p style="text-align: center"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-461" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Cyanidin-300x152.png" alt="" width="300" height="152" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Cyanidin-300x152.png 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Cyanidin-65x33.png 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Cyanidin-225x114.png 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Cyanidin-350x177.png 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Cyanidin.png 459w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center">Chemical Structure of Cyanidin-3-O-rutinoside (C3R)</p>
<p style="text-align: center"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-462" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/C3R-300x132.png" alt="" width="300" height="132" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/C3R-300x132.png 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/C3R-1024x452.png 1024w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/C3R-768x339.png 768w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/C3R-1536x678.png 1536w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/C3R-2048x903.png 2048w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/C3R-65x29.png 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/C3R-225x99.png 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/C3R-350x154.png 350w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></p>
<ol start="2">
<li><strong> Ellagitannins</strong></li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>Sanguiin</li>
<li>Lambertianin</li>
</ul>
<ol start="3">
<li><strong> Ellagic Acid and Derivatives</strong></li>
</ol>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-463 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/e.png" alt="" width="269" height="188" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/e.png 269w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/e-65x45.png 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/e-225x157.png 225w" sizes="(max-width: 269px) 100vw, 269px" /><span style="text-align: initial;font-size: 1em">Black raspberries also have the ability to improve night vision. Some evidence shows that they may also serve as a shield against macular degeneration.</span></p>
<h3><span style="color: #339966"><strong>Mechanisms of Action</strong></span></h3>
<ol>
<li><strong> Antioxidant Activity</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>Even more than wild blueberries, black raspberries have a high level of anthocyanins, which are proven to aid in eye health.</p>
<p><em>Free radical scavenging reaction:</em></p>
<p>Anthocyanin-OH + R• → Anthocyanin-O• + R-H</p>
<p>(Antioxidant) + (Free radical)  → (Stabilized) + (Neutralized)</p>
<ol start="2">
<li><strong> Anti-inflammatory Activity</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>Nitric oxide production in LPS-stimulated RAW264.7 cells treated with C3G and C3R was reduced by 41.9 and 34.4%, respectively. In addition, LPS-induced prostaglandin E2 production was significantly (p&lt;0.05) inhibited by C3G (51.7%) and C3R (58.6%)</p>
<p><em>Reaction pathway:</em></p>
<p>C3G/C3R → ↓ iNOS expression → ↓ NO production → ↓ Inflammation</p>
<p>C3G/C3R → ↓ COX-2 expression → ↓ PGE2 production → ↓ Inflammation</p>
<ol start="3">
<li><strong> Wound Healing Activity</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>The traditional use for wound healing is supported by multiple mechanisms:</p>
<ul>
<li>Antimicrobial effects from phenolic compounds</li>
<li>Anti-inflammatory action reducing tissue damage</li>
<li>Antioxidant protection of healing tissues</li>
<li>Collagen synthesis support from vitamin C</li>
</ul>
<ol start="4">
<li><strong> Cardiovascular Protection</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>C3R induced vasorelaxation concentration-dependently in aortic rings (92% maximum relaxation; EC50: 2.43 ± 0.57 μM) and in perfused-mesenteric arterial bed (61% maximum relaxation; EC50: 25.0 ± 1.26 μM)</p>
<p><em>Vasorelaxation mechanism:</em></p>
<p>C3R → ↑ eNOS activity → ↑ NO production → Vasodilation</p>
<ol start="5">
<li><strong> Anti-cancer Properties</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>Animal studies conducted at Ohio State University showed that mice fed a 5 – 10% diet of black raspberries had an impressive 80% reduction in esophageal cancer. Another animal study at the same university showed a 60 – 80% reduction in colon tumors.</p>
<p><span style="color: #339966"><strong>References</strong></span></p>
<ol>
<li>Elders and Community members of the Cayoose Creek Band of Sekw’el’was</li>
<li>Turner, N. J. (1995). <em data-start="106" data-end="144">Food plants of coastal First Peoples</em> (Royal British Columbia Museum Handbook). UBC Press.</li>
<li>Gunther, E. (1973). <em data-start="259" data-end="358">Ethnobotany of Western Washington: The knowledge and use of indigenous plants by Native Americans</em>. University of Washington Press.</li>
<li>Hummer, K. E. (2010). Rubus pharmacology: Antiquity to the present. <em data-start="501" data-end="518">HortScience, 45</em>(11), 1587–1591. <a href="https://doi.org/10.21273/HORTSCI.45.11.1587" data-start="535" data-end="578">https://doi.org/10.21273/HORTSCI.45.11.1587</a></li>
<li>Moerman, D. E. (2009). <em data-start="643" data-end="707">Native American medicinal plants: An ethnobotanical dictionary</em>. Timber Press</li>
<li>Ludwig, I. A., Mena, P., Calani, L., Borges, G., Pereira-Caro, G., Bresciani, L., Del Rio, D., Lean, M. E. J., &amp; Crozier, A. (2015). New insights into the bioavailability of red raspberry anthocyanins and ellagitannins. <em data-start="984" data-end="1023">Free Radical Biology and Medicine, 89</em>, 758–769. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.freeradbiomed.2015.10.400" data-start="1034" data-end="1085">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.freeradbiomed.2015.10.400</a></li>
<li>Mullen, W., McGinn, J., Lean, M. E. J., MacLean, M. R., Gardner, P., Duthie, G. G., Yokota, T., &amp; Crozier, A. (2002). Ellagitannins, flavonoids, and other phenolics in red raspberries and their contribution to antioxidant capacity and vasorelaxation properties. <em data-start="1389" data-end="1437">Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry, 50</em>(18), 5191–5196. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1021/jf020140n" data-start="1454" data-end="1487">https://doi.org/10.1021/jf020140n</a></li>
<li>Lee, S. G., Kim, B., Yang, Y., Pham, T. X., Park, Y.-K., Manatou, J., Koo, S. I., Chun, O. K., &amp; Lee, J.-Y. (2014). Berry anthocyanins suppress the expression and secretion of proinflammatory mediators in macrophages by inhibiting nuclear translocation of NF-κB independent of NRF2-mediated mechanism. <em data-start="1831" data-end="1872">Journal of Nutritional Biochemistry, 25</em>(4), 404–411. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jnutbio.2013.12.001" data-start="1886" data-end="1931">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jnutbio.2013.12.001</a></li>
<li>Smeriglio, A., Barreca, D., Bellocco, E., &amp; Trombetta, D. (2017). Proanthocyanidins and hydrolysable tannins: Occurrence, dietary intake and pharmacological effects. <em data-start="2139" data-end="2177">British Journal of Pharmacology, 174</em>(11), 1244–1262. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/bph.13630" data-start="2194" data-end="2227">https://doi.org/10.1111/bph.13630</a></li>
<li>Thilavech, T., &amp; Adisakwattana, S. (2019). Cyanidin-3-rutinoside acts as a natural inhibitor of intestinal lipid digestion and absorption. <em data-start="2408" data-end="2456">BMC Complementary and Alternative Medicine, 19</em>, Article 242. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1186/s12906-019-2664-8" data-start="2471" data-end="2512">https://doi.org/10.1186/s12906-019-2664-8</a></li>
<li>Krauze-Baranowska, M., Głód, D., Kula, M., Majdan, M., Hałasa, R., Matkowski, A., Kozłowska, W., &amp; Kawiak, A. (2014). Chemical composition and biological activity of <em data-start="2720" data-end="2734">Rubus idaeus</em> shoots – A traditional herbal remedy of Eastern Europe. <em data-start="2791" data-end="2839">BMC Complementary and Alternative Medicine, 14</em>, Article 480. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1186/1472-6882-14-480" data-start="2854" data-end="2894">https://doi.org/10.1186/1472-6882-14-480</a></li>
</ol>
</section>
