			<section data-type="chapter" class="standard post-204 chapter type-chapter status-publish hentry">
		<header>
				<h1 class="entry-title">
			<span>54</span> Pearly Everlasting		</h1>
										</header>
		<p><span style="color: #339966"><strong>Names</strong></span></p>
<p><strong>Common name</strong> &#8211; Pearly Everlasting</p>
<p><strong>Scientific name</strong> &#8211;<em> Anaphalis margaritacea</em></p>
<p><strong>Other names</strong> &#8211; Western Pearly Everlasting</p>
<h3><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-842 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture41-300x297.png" alt="" width="300" height="297" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture41-300x297.png 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture41-150x150.png 150w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture41-65x64.png 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture41-225x223.png 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture41-350x347.png 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture41.png 624w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /><span style="color: #339966"><strong>Traditional Indigenous Uses </strong></span></h3>
<p style="text-align: justify">The Elders speak of Pearly Everlasting as a gentle yet powerful healer, a plant that carried the strength of endurance, living up to its name. Its soft, silvery leaves and white blossoms were gathered and dried for medicine, especially to help those with sickness of the lungs and chest. The people would smoke the dried leaves and flowers to calm coughs, ease asthma, and clear the breathing when colds took hold. Sometimes it was brewed into a hot tea or used in steam baths to relieve the pain of rheumatism and aching joints, its warmth drawing the stiffness from the body. When the skin was wounded or burned, poultices made from the plant were laid over the sores, cooling the pain and helping the flesh to heal.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify" data-start="910" data-end="1680">The tea was also taken to settle the stomach and treat diarrhea or dysentery, bringing relief to those weakened by sickness. For headaches, the steam from its infusion was inhaled to clear the head and soothe the mind, while a flower wash was used to cleanse irritated eyes and treat infections. Pearly Everlasting was even used in ceremonies, sometimes smoked alone or mixed with other plants as a gentle tobacco substitute, connecting breath and spirit. Its soothing nature reduced swelling and inflammation, whether taken as tea or used as a wash.</p>
<h3><span style="color: #339966"><strong>Biochemical Basis for Medicinal Properties</strong></span></h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Essential Oil Components</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>Major compounds included isocaryophyllene, caryophyllene oxide, geranyl-α-terpinene, α-pinene, γ-muurolene, δ-cadinene, humulene, and copaene.</p>
<p>(i) Terpenoids</p>
<p>Caryophyllene Oxide (C₁₅H₂₄O)</p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-844 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/1-7.png" alt="" width="260" height="276" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/1-7.png 260w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/1-7-65x69.png 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/1-7-225x239.png 225w" sizes="(max-width: 260px) 100vw, 260px" /></p>
<p>(ii) α-Pinene (C₁₀H₁₆)</p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-845 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/2-7.png" alt="" width="243" height="243" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/2-7.png 243w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/2-7-150x150.png 150w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/2-7-65x65.png 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/2-7-225x225.png 225w" sizes="(max-width: 243px) 100vw, 243px" /></p>
<ol start="2">
<li><strong> Flavonoids</strong></li>
</ol>
<p><em>General Flavonoid Structure (Quercetin-type)</em></p>
<ol start="3">
<li><strong> Triterpenoids</strong></li>
</ol>
<p><em>General Triterpenoid Structure</em></p>
<ol start="4">
<li><strong> Chlorinated Polyacetylene</strong></li>
</ol>
<p><em>(E)-5-chloro-2-(octa-2,4,6-triynylidene)-5,6-dihydro-2H-pyran &#8211; </em>A thirteen-carbon chlorinated polyacetylene found in the roots of <em>Anaphalis</em> species; this compound is of particular interest due to its structural similarity to known antibacterial and antifungal compounds.</p>
<h3><span style="color: #339966"><strong>Chemical Reactions and Mechanisms</strong></span></h3>
<p><strong>Anti-inflammatory Mechanism (Caryophyllene Oxide)</strong></p>
<p>Caryophyllene Oxide + CB₂ Receptor → Anti-inflammatory cascade → Reduced prostaglandin synthesis</p>
<p><strong>Antimicrobial Action (Essential Oils)</strong></p>
<p>Terpene compounds + Bacterial cell membrane → Membrane disruption + Enzyme inhibition → Cell death</p>
<p><strong>Expectorant Mechanism</strong></p>
<p>The combination of volatile compounds stimulates mucus secretion:</p>
<p>α-Pinene + Respiratory epithelium → Increased ciliary action + Mucus liquefaction</p>
<p><strong>Astringent Properties</strong></p>
<p>Tannins and flavonoids provide astringent effects:</p>
<p>Flavonoid-OH + Protein → Protein precipitation + Tissue contraction</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span style="color: #339966"><strong>References</strong></span></p>
<p>1) Elders and Community members of the Cayoose Creek Band of Sekw’el’was</p>
<p>2) Bhattarai, N. K. (1992). Folk herbal remedies for gynaecological complaints in Central Nepal. <em>International Journal of Pharmacognosy, 30</em>(4), 283–292. <a href="https://doi.org/10.3109/13880209209082835">https://doi.org/10.3109/13880209209082835</a></p>
<p>3) Khatri, D., Panthi, M. P., &amp; Shrestha, K. (2024). Essential oil composition and antimicrobial activity of <em>Anaphalis margaritacea</em> collected from the Himalayas. <em>Chemistry of Natural Compounds, 60</em>(3), 411–418. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10600-024-04031-8">https://doi.org/10.1007/s10600-024-04031-8</a></p>
<p>4) Kim, S., Kang, S. W., &amp; Lee, H. (2019). Antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties of <em>Anaphalis margaritacea</em> extract in vitro and in vivo. <em>Journal of Ethnopharmacology, 239,</em> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jep.2019.111890">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jep.2019.111890</a></p>
<p>5) Park, Y. J., Jang, T. S., &amp; Lee, S. R. (2017). Identification of polyacetylenes with antibacterial and antifungal properties from <em>Anaphalis margaritacea</em>. <em>Natural Product Research, 31</em>(10), 1158–1163. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/14786419.2016.1263851">https://doi.org/10.1080/14786419.2016.1263851</a></p>
<p>6) Turner, N. J., &amp; Hebda, R. J. (1990). Contemporary use of bark for medicine by two Salishan Native elders of southeast Vancouver Island, Canada. <em>Journal of Ethnopharmacology, 29</em>(1), 59–72. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0378-8741(90)90038-Q">https://doi.org/10.1016/0378-8741(90)90038-Q</a></p>
<p>7) S. Department of Agriculture, Forest Service. (2025). <em>Plant of the week: Pearly everlasting (Anaphalis margaritacea).</em> <a href="https://www.fs.usda.gov/wildflowers/plant-of-the-week/anaphalis_margaritacea.shtml">https://www.fs.usda.gov/wildflowers/plant-of-the-week/anaphalis_margaritacea.shtml</a></p>
<p>8) Wang, Z., Li, R., &amp; Guo, Y. (2021). Bioactive terpenoids and flavonoids from <em>Anaphalis margaritacea</em> and their pharmacological properties. <em>Frontiers in Pharmacology, 12,</em> <a href="https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2021.725631">https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2021.725631</a></p>
</section>
