			<section data-type="chapter" class="standard post-242 chapter type-chapter status-publish hentry">
		<header>
				<h1 class="entry-title">
			<span>69</span> Thread-Leaved Sandwort		</h1>
										</header>
		<p><strong><span style="color: #339966">Names </span></strong></p>
<p><strong>Common name</strong> &#8211; Thread-Leaved Sandwort</p>
<p><strong>Scientific name</strong> &#8211; <em>Arenaria capillaris</em></p>
<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-370 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-3-300x225.jpg" alt="" width="300" height="225" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-3-300x225.jpg 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-3-768x577.jpg 768w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-3-65x49.jpg 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-3-225x169.jpg 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-3-350x263.jpg 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-3.jpg 799w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-371 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-9-300x200.png" alt="" width="300" height="200" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-9-300x200.png 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-9-65x43.png 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-9-225x150.png 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-9-350x233.png 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-9.png 624w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></p>
<p><span style="color: #339966"><strong>General Information</strong></span></p>
<p>Thread-Leaved Sandwort (<em>Arenaria capillaris</em>) is a small perennial plant with hair-like leaves and small white flowers, native to western North America. However, there is no specific documented traditional medicinal uses of this particular species. There is also limited ethnobotanical research on this specific species.</p>
<p><em>Any medicinal applications should be considered theoretical and potentially unsafe. Traditional plant medicines should only be used under guidance from qualified practitioners familiar with both the plants and traditional preparation methods. </em></p>
</section>
</div><!-- #content -->
