	<div id="content" class="site-content" tabindex="-1">
			<section data-type="chapter" class="standard post-248 chapter type-chapter status-publish hentry">
		<header>
				<h1 class="entry-title">
			<span>71</span> Tufted Prairie Aster		</h1>
										</header>
		<p><strong><span style="color: #339966">Names</span></strong><br />
<strong>Common name</strong> &#8211; Tufted Prairie Aster<br />
<strong>Scientific name</strong> &#8211; <em>Symphyotrichum caespitosum</em><br />
<strong>Other names</strong> &#8211; White Heather Aster</p>
<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-352 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-7-300x229.png" alt="" width="300" height="229" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-7-300x229.png 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-7-65x50.png 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-7-225x172.png 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-7-350x268.png 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture1-7.png 624w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-353 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-8-300x269.png" alt="" width="300" height="269" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-8-300x269.png 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-8-65x58.png 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-8-225x202.png 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-8-350x314.png 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture2-8.png 624w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-354 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-4-300x125.png" alt="" width="300" height="125" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-4-300x125.png 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-4-65x27.png 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-4-225x94.png 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-4-350x146.png 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture3-4.png 624w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></p>
<h3><strong><span style="color: #339966">Traditional Indigenous Uses</span></strong></h3>
<p style="text-align: justify">The roots were carefully dug, dried, and pounded into a soft powder to stop bleeding from cuts and scrapes. This medicine was applied directly to the skin, helping to close wounds and prevent infection.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify">The whole plant was often prepared in infusions or decoctions. When combined with other medicinal herbs, a tea made from the Aster helped to bring down fevers and cool the body during sickness. The same decoction could be used to wash wounds, keeping them clean and speeding healing. Dried and powdered, the plant was mixed into a salve or paste to soothe abrasions and skin irritations. Its crushed leaves and flowers were also placed directly on burns, insect bites, or sores, easing pain and calming inflammation.</p>
<p style="text-align: justify">The crushed blossoms of Tufted Prairie Aster were burned, and the smoke gently inhaled to clear the chest and aid breathing.</p>
<h3><span style="color: #339966"><strong style="color: #339966">Biochemical Basis for Medicinal Properties</strong></span></h3>
<p><strong>Key Bioactive Compounds in Aster Species</strong></p>
<p>Saponin compounds, responsible for many pharmacological activities, are quite abundant in Aster plants. The phytochemical constituents identified in medicinal plants include flavonoids, alkaloids, glycosides, phenols, saponins, steroids, and terpenoids, with flavonoids, alkaloids, and phenols being the most abundant.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Triterpenoid Saponins</strong></li>
</ol>
<p style="text-align: center">General Triterpenoid Saponin Structure</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-357 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/sap-300x153.gif" alt="" width="300" height="153" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/sap-300x153.gif 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/sap-65x33.gif 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/sap-225x115.gif 225w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /><span style="text-decoration: underline">Mechanisms of Action</span></p>
<ol>
<li>Hemostatic effects: Saponins can cause protein precipitation and vasoconstriction</li>
<li>Anti-inflammatory: Modulation of inflammatory mediators</li>
<li>Antimicrobial: Membrane disruption in pathogens</li>
</ol>
<p><em>Chemical Reaction (Protein precipitation for wound healing):</em></p>
<p>Saponin + Blood proteins → Saponin-protein complex (clot formation)</p>
<ol start="2">
<li><strong>Flavonoids</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>Compounds isolated from Aster species include quercetin and kaempferol.</p>
<p style="text-align: center">Quercetin Structure</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-358 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Q-300x300.jpg" alt="" width="300" height="300" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Q-300x300.jpg 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Q-1024x1024.jpg 1024w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Q-150x150.jpg 150w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Q-768x768.jpg 768w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Q-1536x1536.jpg 1536w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Q-2048x2048.jpg 2048w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Q-65x65.jpg 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Q-225x225.jpg 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Q-350x350.jpg 350w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /><span style="text-decoration: underline">Mechanisms</span></p>
<ol>
<li>Antioxidant activity: Free radical scavenging</li>
<li>Anti-inflammatory: COX and LOX enzyme inhibition</li>
<li>Wound healing: Collagen synthesis promotion</li>
</ol>
<p><em>Chemical Reaction (Antioxidant mechanism):</em></p>
<p>Quercetin-OH + R• → Quercetin-O• + RH</p>
<p>(Free radical neutralization)</p>
<ol start="3">
<li><strong>Phenolic Compounds</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>Phenolic compounds are among the most important phytochemicals in medicinal plants.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline">General Mechanism</span></p>
<ol>
<li>Astringent effects: Tannin-like protein precipitation</li>
<li>Antimicrobial: Cell membrane disruption</li>
<li>Hemostatic: Vasoconstriction and clotting promotion</li>
</ol>
<p><em>Chemical Reaction (Astringent mechanism):</em></p>
<p>Phenolic-OH + Protein-NH2 → H-bonded complex</p>
<p>(Tissue tightening and bleeding control)</p>
<ol start="4">
<li><strong>Phenylpropanoids</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>Aster species contain phenylpropanoids including lignans.</p>
<p style="text-align: center">Basic Phenylpropanoid Structure:</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-360 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/use-300x87.png" alt="" width="300" height="87" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/use-300x87.png 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/use-1024x298.png 1024w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/use-768x223.png 768w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/use-65x19.png 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/use-225x65.png 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/use-350x102.png 350w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/use.png 1104w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /><span style="font-size: 1em;text-align: initial">Mechanisms</span></p>
<ol>
<li>Antimicrobial: Cell wall disruption</li>
<li>Anti-inflammatory: Enzyme inhibition</li>
<li>Wound healing: Tissue repair promotion</li>
</ol>
<h3><span style="color: #339966"><strong>Specific Mechanisms for Traditional Uses</strong></span></h3>
<p><strong><span style="text-decoration: underline">Hemostatic (Bleeding Control) Activity</span></strong></p>
<p>Active Components: Tannins, saponins, flavonoids</p>
<p>Mechanism:</p>
<ol>
<li>Vasoconstriction: Smooth muscle contraction in blood vessels</li>
<li>Platelet aggregation: Enhanced clotting cascade</li>
<li>Protein precipitation: Formation of protective barriers</li>
</ol>
<p>Chemical Process:</p>
<p>Tannins + Damaged tissue proteins → Cross-linked protein matrix</p>
<p>(Physical barrier formation)</p>
<p><strong><span style="text-decoration: underline">Anti-inflammatory Activity</span></strong></p>
<p>Active Components: Flavonoids (quercetin, kaempferol), phenolic acids</p>
<p>Mechanism:</p>
<ol>
<li>Enzyme inhibition: COX-1, COX-2, and 5-LOX inhibition</li>
<li>Cytokine modulation: Reduced IL-1β, TNF-α production</li>
<li>Free radical scavenging: Oxidative stress reduction</li>
</ol>
<p>Chemical Reaction:</p>
<p>Flavonoid + Cyclooxygenase → Flavonoid-enzyme complex (inactive)</p>
<p><strong><span style="text-decoration: underline">Antimicrobial Activity</span></strong></p>
<p>Active Components: Saponins, phenolic compounds, essential oils</p>
<p>Mechanism:</p>
<ol>
<li>Membrane disruption: Cholesterol interaction in bacterial membranes</li>
<li>Protein denaturation: Disruption of cellular enzymes</li>
<li>DNA binding: Interference with replication</li>
</ol>
<h3><span style="color: #ff0000"><strong>Important Considerations</strong></span></h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Limited specific research</strong> on Symphyotrichum caespitosum phytochemistry</li>
<li><strong>Extrapolation from related species</strong> &#8211; biochemical data primarily from other Aster species</li>
<li><strong>No clinical validation</strong> of traditional uses</li>
<li><strong>Potential variability</strong> in bioactive compound concentrations based on geographic location and harvest time</li>
</ol>
<p><span style="background-color: #ffff00;color: #ff0000"><strong>Note</strong>: </span>While traditional uses are documented, modern clinical studies validating the efficacy and safety of Tufted Prairie Aster for medicinal purposes are lacking. Traditional plant medicines should only be used under qualified guidance.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong><span style="color: #339966">References</span></strong></p>
<p>1) Elders and Community members of the Cayoose Creek Band of Sekw’el’was</p>
<p>2) United States Department of Agriculture, Natural Resources Conservation Service. (2006). <em data-start="260" data-end="330">White panicle aster (Symphyotrichum lanceolatum) &#8211; Plant Fact Sheet.</em> USDA NRCS.</p>
<p>3) United States Department of Agriculture, Natural Resources Conservation Service. (2006). <em data-start="544" data-end="605">White heath aster (Symphyotrichum ericoides) — Plant Guide.</em> USDA NRCS.</p>
<p>4) Shao, Y., Zhou, B. N., Lin, L. Z., &amp; Cordell, G. A. (1995). Triterpene saponins from <em data-start="807" data-end="826">Aster yunnanensis</em>. <em data-start="828" data-end="848">Phytochemistry, 38</em>(6), 1487–1492. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0031-9422(94)00794-T" data-start="864" data-end="908">https://doi.org/10.1016/0031-9422(94)00794-T</a></p>
<p>5) Jan, R., Khan, M., Asaf, S., Lubna, Asif, S., &amp; Kim, K.-M. (2022). Bioactivity and therapeutic potential of kaempferol and quercetin: New insights for plant and human health. <em data-start="1126" data-end="1138">Plants, 11</em>(19), 2623. <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/plants11192623" data-start="1150" data-end="1188">https://doi.org/10.3390/plants11192623</a></p>
<p>6) Marcińczyk, N., Gromotowicz-Popławska, A., Tomczyk, M., &amp; Chabielska, E. (2022). Tannins as hemostasis modulators. <em data-start="1346" data-end="1377">Frontiers in Pharmacology, 12</em>, 806891. <a href="https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2021.806891" data-start="1387" data-end="1428">https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2021.806891</a></p>
</section>
