			<section data-type="chapter" class="standard post-495 chapter type-chapter status-publish hentry">
		<header>
				<h1 class="entry-title">
			<span>77</span> Wild Asparagus		</h1>
										</header>
		<p><strong><span style="color: #339966">Names</span></strong></p>
<p><strong>Common Name</strong> &#8211; Wild Asparagus</p>
<p><strong>Scientific Name</strong>: <em>Asparagus officinalis</em></p>
<p><strong>Other Names:</strong> Sparrowgrass, garden asparagus</p>
<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="size-full wp-image-498 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture6.jpg" alt="" width="203" height="268" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture6.jpg 203w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture6-65x86.jpg 65w" sizes="(max-width: 203px) 100vw, 203px" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center"><strong>Figure 1. Mature asparagus on a trail near Kamloops, BC. </strong></p>
<p style="text-align: center">Image Source: <a href="https://kamloopstrails.net/news/wild-asparagus/">https://kamloopstrails.net/news/wild-asparagus/</a></p>
<p>The young shoots of wild asparagus are an important food source. While low in calories, asparagus contains many vitamins and minerals and is high in protein and dietary fiber which is important for digestive health and may help reduce the risk of diabetes. Wild asparagus also contains saponins, flavonoids and sterols.</p>
<p style="text-align: center"><img decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-503 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture7-1-300x224.jpg" alt="" width="300" height="224" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture7-1-300x224.jpg 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture7-1-65x49.jpg 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture7-1-225x168.jpg 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture7-1.jpg 328w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /><strong style="text-align: initial;font-size: 1em">Figure 2. Young Asparagus Shoots in the Similkameen region.</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: center">Image Source: <a href="https://infotel.ca/inwine/okanagan-asparagus-season-is-underway/it82118">https://infotel.ca/inwine/okanagan-asparagus-season-is-underway/it82118</a></p>
<p>The dietary fibre in asparagus contains high quantities of pectin (structure shown below), as well as cellulose and lignin. It is also interesting to note that, although the young shoots are eaten because the taste and texture becomes less palatable as the stalks age, the young shoots actually contain more nutrients than the older shoots do as well.</p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="size-medium wp-image-504 aligncenter" src="http://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture8-300x138.jpg" alt="" width="300" height="138" srcset="https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture8-300x138.jpg 300w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture8-65x30.jpg 65w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture8-225x104.jpg 225w, https://indigenousmedicinescayoosecreek.pressbooks.tru.ca/wp-content/uploads/sites/188/2025/09/Picture8.jpg 351w" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" /></p>
<p>Although edible, handling wild asparagus can cause dermatitis and so should be collected and handled with care. It is also possible to have an allergy to asparagus which may result in asthma- like symptoms.</p>
<p><strong><span style="color: #339966">References</span></strong></p>
<p>[1] Chelh TC, Rincon-Cervera MA, Gomez-Mercado F, Lopez-Ruiz R, Gallon-Bedoya M, Ezzaitouni M, Guil-Guerrero 2023. Wild Asparagus Shoots Constitute a Healthy Source of Bioactive Compounds [Internet]. Molecules 28(15). [Accessed November 20 2024]</p>
<p>[2] Tabar AI, Alvarez-Puebla MJ, Gomez B, Sanchez-Monge R, Garcia BE, Echechipia S, Olaguibel JM, Salcedo 2004. Diversity of Asparagus Allergy: Clinical and Immunological Features [Internet]. Clinical Exp. Allergy, 34(1). [Accessed April 19 2025]. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14720273/">https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14720273/</a></p>
<p>[3] Guo Q, Wang N, Liu H, Li Z, Lu L, Wang C. (2020). The Bioactive Compounds and Biological Functions of Asparagus offincinalis – A Review. Journal of Functional Foods 65. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jff.2019.103727">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jff.2019.103727</a></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
</section>
